{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 1, "segment_title": "Why Integrate Along the y-axis?", "segment_objective": "Recognize when a region is more naturally described by horizontal strips and explain why integrating with respect to y is advantageous.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 10.07, "diag_hint": "conic_figure", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 36977, "cache_hit_tokens": 5248, "output_tokens": 624}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Why Integrate Along the y-axis?", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "When a region is bounded by a vertical line (like the y-axis) and a curve, horizontal strips are more natural.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "Horizontal strip: height dy, length x = g(y). Sweep from y = c to y = d.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "diagram", "template": "conic_figure", "params": {"kind": "parabola", "a": 1, "b": 1}, "caption": "Parabola y^2 = 4x opening to the right"}], "model_named_templates": ["conic_figure"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 2, "segment_title": "The Horizontal Strip Formula", "segment_objective": "State and apply the formula A = \u222b x dy for a region bounded by a curve, the y-axis, and horizontal lines.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 7.57, "diag_hint": "number_line", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37467, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 766}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Area by Horizontal Strips (Integrating with respect to y)", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "For a region bounded by the y-axis, a curve x = g(y), and horizontal lines y = c and y = d, the area is found by summing horizontal strips of length x and thickness dy.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "formula", "latex": "A = \\int_{c}^{d} x \\, dy = \\int_{c}^{d} g(y) \\, dy", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "text", "text": "Each horizontal strip has length x (distance from y-axis to curve) and thickness dy. Sum from y = c to y = d.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 5, "type": "diagram", "template": "number_line", "params": {"intervals": [{"lo": 0, "hi": 2, "lo_closed": true, "hi_closed": true, "label": "y: 0 to 2"}], "title": "Limits of integration along y"}, "caption": "Sweep y from c to d"}], "model_named_templates": ["number_line"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "formula", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 3, "segment_title": "Choosing Limits and Handling Signs", "segment_objective": "Determine correct limits of integration from given boundaries and handle cases where the curve crosses the y-axis.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 8.42, "diag_hint": "number_line", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 36841, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 885}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Choosing Limits and Handling Signs", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "Limits of integration are the y-values of the horizontal boundaries (the lines y = c and y = d).", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "If the curve x = g(y) crosses the y-axis inside the interval, the integral gives signed area; positive and negative parts cancel.", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "text", "text": "To find geometric area, split the integral at the crossing point and take absolute value of each part.", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 5, "type": "diagram", "template": "number_line", "params": {"title": "Splitting at the crossing y = 0", "intervals": [{"lo": -1, "hi": 0, "lo_closed": true, "hi_closed": false, "label": "A1 = \u222b|y\u00b3|dy"}, {"lo": 0, "hi": 1, "lo_closed": true, "hi_closed": true, "label": "A2 = \u222by\u00b3dy"}]}, "caption": "Split the integral at y = 0, the crossing point"}], "model_named_templates": ["number_line"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "text", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 4, "segment_title": "Worked Example: Parabola and y-axis", "segment_objective": "Compute the area of a region bounded by a parabola, the y-axis, and horizontal lines using integration with respect to y.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 6.63, "diag_hint": "conic_figure", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37117, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 640}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Worked Example: Area Bounded by x = y^2, y-axis, y=0, y=2", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "The region is bounded by the parabola x = y^2 (opening to the right), the y-axis (x=0), and the horizontal lines y=0 and y=2.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "Since the boundaries are horizontal lines, use horizontal strips: each strip has height dy and length x = y^2.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "formula", "latex": "A = \\int_{0}^{2} x \\, dy = \\int_{0}^{2} y^2 \\, dy", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "diagram", "template": "conic_figure", "params": {"kind": "parabola", "a": 1, "b": 1}, "caption": "Parabola x = y^2 opening to the right, bounded by y = 0 and y = 2"}], "model_named_templates": ["conic_figure"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "text", "formula", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 5, "segment_title": "When to Choose dy over dx", "segment_objective": "Decide whether to integrate with respect to x or y based on the geometry of the region.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 8.71, "diag_hint": "conic_figure", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37105, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 787}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 2, "type": "heading", "text": "Choosing dy vs dx", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "Decision rule: If the region is bounded by horizontal lines and the curve is easily expressed as x = g(y), integrate with respect to y. If bounded by vertical lines and the curve is y = f(x), integrate with respect to x.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "note", "text": "Sketch first! The shape of the region tells you which strip orientation is natural.", "emphasis": "high"}, {"seq": 5, "type": "text", "text": "Example: Region bounded by y^2 = 4x and x = 4.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 6, "type": "diagram", "template": "conic_figure", "params": {"kind": "parabola", "a": 1, "b": 1}, "caption": "Parabola y^2 = 4x opening to the right, bounded by x = 4"}], "model_named_templates": ["conic_figure"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "note", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 6, "segment_title": "Area Between Two Curves Using dy", "segment_objective": "Compute the area between two curves by integrating with respect to y, using right minus left.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 6.72, "diag_hint": "conic_figure", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37194, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 648}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Area Between Two Curves Using dy", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "When integrating with respect to y, the length of a horizontal strip is the difference between the right curve and the left curve: x_right - x_left.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "formula", "latex": "A = \\int_{c}^{d} (x_{\\text{right}} - x_{\\text{left}}) \\, dy", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "text", "text": "Example: Find the area bounded by the parabola y^2 = 4x and the line y = 2x - 4.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 5, "type": "diagram", "template": "conic_figure", "params": {"kind": "parabola", "a": 1, "b": 1}, "caption": "Parabola y^2 = 4x opening to the right, with the line y = 2x - 4 cutting across it."}], "model_named_templates": ["conic_figure"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "formula", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 7, "segment_title": "Symmetry and Efficiency", "segment_objective": "Use symmetry about the x-axis to simplify area calculations when integrating with respect to y.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 7.07, "diag_hint": "conic_figure", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37155, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 797}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Symmetry and Efficiency", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "When a region is symmetric about the x-axis, compute the area of the upper half and double it.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "Example: Region bounded by y^2 = 4x and x = 4. The parabola opens right, symmetric about x-axis.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "text", "text": "For the upper half, y ranges from 0 to 4. Express x as a function of y: x = y^2/4.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "diagram", "template": "conic_figure", "params": {"kind": "parabola", "a": 1, "b": 1}, "caption": "Parabola y^2 = 4x, symmetric about the x-axis"}], "model_named_templates": ["conic_figure"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "text", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
{"chapter": "Application of Integrals", "subtopic_key": "area-by-integration-along-the-y-axis", "plan_id": "da24c01c-c49a-4403-918b-5a76a31df514", "session_id": "f856b5a0-2546-44ec-9608-4a192ad11115", "segment_index": 8, "segment_title": "Common Pitfalls and Exam Strategy", "segment_objective": "Identify and avoid common mistakes in area problems involving integration along the y-axis.", "has_example_diagram_svg": true, "harness_error": null, "latency_s": 8.48, "diag_hint": "labeled_axes_plot", "board_slot": "svg_precomputed", "tokens": {"input_tokens": 37374, "cache_hit_tokens": 33280, "output_tokens": 990}, "model_id": "deepseek-v4-flash", "llm_ok": true, "turn_error": null, "sanitize_calls": [], "prior_payload_block": "", "decline": null, "model_raw_board_events": [{"seq": 1, "type": "heading", "text": "Common Pitfalls and Exam Strategy", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 2, "type": "text", "text": "Pitfall 1: Wrong variable. A region walled by the y-axis and horizontal lines wants $\\int x \\, dy$, not $\\int y \\, dx$. Forcing dx creates needless casework.", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 3, "type": "text", "text": "Pitfall 2: Wrong limits. Limits are the boundary lines or intersection points, not arbitrary numbers. For a curve like $x = y^2$ bounded by $y = 0$ and $y = 2$, limits are 0 and 2.", "emphasis": "normal"}, {"seq": 4, "type": "text", "text": "Pitfall 3: Sign errors. If the curve goes below the axis, the raw integral is negative; if it crosses, pieces cancel. Always sketch first and use $|x| \\, dy$, splitting at roots.", "emphasis": "key"}, {"seq": 5, "type": "diagram", "template": "labeled_axes_plot", "params": {"x_label": "y", "y_label": "x", "curve_points": [[-1, -1], [-0.5, -0.125], [0, 0], [0.5, 0.125], [1, 1]], "annotations": [{"x": 0, "y": 0, "text": "split at y = 0"}, {"x": -1, "y": -1, "text": "y = -1"}, {"x": 1, "y": 1, "text": "y = 1"}], "title": "x = y^3: split at root y = 0"}, "caption": "Curve x = y^3 crosses the axis at y = 0 \u2014 split here."}], "model_named_templates": ["labeled_axes_plot"], "declines": [], "emitted_event_types": ["heading", "text", "text", "text", "diagram"], "fired": false, "widget": null, "params": null, "node_list": [], "emitted_instead": "svg", "svg_source": "segment_example_diagram_svg", "correct_widget": false, "server_validated": false, "non_fire_reason": "widget_cannot_express_concept", "sane": null, "client_validate": null}
